@nascheme just to clarify this issue is slightly different in that it's a user callback that exposed the bug

I would say we don't support callback doing that.  It is quite limited what it is safe for the callback to.  E.g. you can't run user level Python code from there and the APIs you are allowed to call is very limited.  Basically the callback is meant for CPython to use, not for extensions to use.
Oh, I see PyUnstable_GC_VisitObjects exists.  Oh dear.

I think you just haven't yet found the new bugs that this PR introduces. The original bug report was only found via fuzzing. I suspect that if you keep fuzzing you will find different ones with this PR.
The problems is that we have code in the form:
Py_CLEAR(tstate->var1);
Py_CLEAR(tstate->var2);
...
Py_CLEAR(tstate->varN);

The underlying problem is that when you clear tstate->var2 you may execute Python code that re-initializes tstate->var2 or variables cleared earlier like tstate->var1. If you move around the order of clearing, you change the possible bugs.
For example, if you move tstate->threading_local_key to the end, you now have potential leaks where thread local variable destructors set things like tstate->asyncio_running_loop

I then profiled and found the repeated method binding was actually most of the runtime so built this PR.

I was surprised to find that print() creates a bound method. How about avoiding the creation of a bound method with the following patch?
diff --git a/Objects/fileobject.c b/Objects/fileobject.c
index 79aa8889c1a..deaff193457 100644
--- a/Objects/fileobject.c
+++ b/Objects/fileobject.c
@@ -106,27 +106,21 @@ PyFile_GetLine(PyObject *f, int n)
 int
 PyFile_WriteObject(PyObject *v, PyObject *f, int flags)
 {
-    PyObject *writer, *value, *result;
-
     if (f == NULL) {
         PyErr_SetString(PyExc_TypeError, "writeobject with NULL file");
         return -1;
     }
-    writer = PyObject_GetAttr(f, &_Py_ID(write));
-    if (writer == NULL)
-        return -1;
-    if (flags & Py_PRINT_RAW) {
-        value = PyObject_Str(v);
-    }
-    else
-        value = PyObject_Repr(v);
+
+    /* str()/repr() may remove the last external reference to the stream. */
+    Py_INCREF(f);
+    PyObject *value = (flags & Py_PRINT_RAW) ? PyObject_Str(v) : PyObject_Repr(v);
     if (value == NULL) {
-        Py_DECREF(writer);
+        Py_DECREF(f);
         return -1;
     }
-    result = PyObject_CallOneArg(writer, value);
+    PyObject *result = PyObject_CallMethodOneArg(f, &_Py_ID(write), value);
     Py_DECREF(value);
-    Py_DECREF(writer);
+    Py_DECREF(f);
     if (result == NULL)
         return -1;
     Py_DECREF(result);

All checks are green, including the Windows builds.
@gaogaotiantian, could you review this? It only touches Lib/pdb.py: the interrupt listener stops half-closing the socket. A half-closed connection waits in FIN_WAIT_2, and Windows resets it after 120 seconds, which drops the interrupt channel of an idle remote session (gh-160683). The author of that line confirmed that it only documented the one-way direction: #160683 (comment)
The new test starts the listener in a subprocess and asserts that the accepted socket never sees EOF. It fails in 147 ms without the change.

